Summary of threads written into analysis.md:

1. "Give me 20 things" seeded list-generation experiment — the sidenote's
   LLM, seeded with a phonetic pattern (butterfly/dragonfly), completes
   the pattern, then drifts outward through flying bugs -> general bugs
   -> flying mammals, collapsing further into general mammals once "50
   more" is requested. Tied to the paper's "Physical things" section
   (repeated unseeded "give me 20 things, anything" prompts) and its
   Waddington's-epigenetic-landscape metaphor, plus the "Mechanical
   things" complexity-gradient prompt. Read as an inverse probe of the
   same landscape (escaping a narrow seeded pocket vs. the paper's
   observation of where an unseeded prompt lands) and as testing a
   mechanism — generic-category collapse under sustained list extension —
   that the paper's landscape metaphor implies but never runs. Flagged
   two gaps against the paper's own methodology: no reported model
   version/session date (Benzon logs his precisely) and no repeated-run
   check for rigidity (Benzon reruns his own list prompt three times,
   including after logging out, for exactly this reason), plus the
   unaddressed role of temperature, which Benzon discusses explicitly.

2. 20 Questions abstract/concrete bias experiment — the sidenote runs a
   full hypothesis -> takeaway -> issue -> fix -> result arc: hypothesizes
   ChatGPT's first question is always abstract/concrete and, since
   abstract words are rarer, disambiguating them takes fewer steps;
   generalizes to "binary questions favor the less frequent branch";
   catches its own confound (the concrete/abstract ratio in the
   experiment doesn't match the English dictionary); fixes it via a
   custom equal-split dictionary disclosed to the model; finds no
   significant bias in the corrected run. Tied to the paper's "ChatGPT
   Plays 20 Questions" section and its six-target table (bicycle, squid,
   apple vs. justice, evolution, truth), which is the source of the
   "abstract does better" finding the sidenote sets out to explain.
   Checked the hypothesis against the actual transcripts: the first
   question is consistently about animacy/tangibility, not
   abstract/concrete, which only emerges as question 2-3 — noted as an
   overclaim in the sidenote's stated mechanism. Identified the "issue"
   step as a direct answer to a sampling question Benzon poses himself
   but leaves open ("what universe are we sampling and how do we choose
   our samples?"), while noting the fix's protocol change (disclosing the
   candidate list to the model) tests a different game than Benzon's
   open-world search, so the null result doesn't cleanly refute his
   finding. Flagged the corrected experiment's unstated sample size as a
   remaining gap, mirroring the same small-sample caveat Benzon raises
   about his own six-targets-times-two data.

Both threads are noted as anecdotal/single-session probes, with a
cross-thread note that Thread 2's self-correction is the more rigorous of
